+/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * Hot cores (Demon's Souls NOTES fix 132; DES_HOTCORES=0 = off)
+ *
+ * The frame is a hand-off chain: the HighGraphics PPU thread fills a command queue and one SPU
+ * task (the SPURS command dispatcher, a pooled host thread) drains it. Uncapped, the dispatcher is
+ * busy all the time and HighGraphics waits on it. Left to the scheduler the two sometimes share a
+ * physical core (SMT siblings: Latria 4-2 48 instead of 73 fps) or have a sibling busy with other
+ * game threads. Each gets a whole physical core of its own here, and every other thread of the
+ * process is kept off those two: Latria uncapped 72.9 -> 77.4 fps on a Ryzen 7 5800X.
+ *
+ * Windows CPU sets: the process default set (every thread that has no set of its own, the driver's
+ * threads included) is all usable CPUs but the claimed hot cores; a hot thread selects its core. A
+ * core leaves the default set only once its slot is claimed, so a slot nobody claims (the dispatcher
+ * with DES_DISPQ_NATIVE=0) costs nothing. Reserved: two cores of the fastest efficiency class (Intel
+ * P-cores), the best by Windows' scheduling class (preferred cores) and then the highest-numbered,
+ * within the process affinity mask (fix 65's L3 pin). Only with at least 6 usable physical cores of
+ * that class, so four remain for the rest; smaller CPUs, hybrid CPUs with fewer P-cores, and
+ * machines with several processor groups keep the scheduler's placement. Parked cores count: parking
+ * changes at run time, and the scheduler deals with it.
+ * ps3_hot_thread(slot) is called by the thread itself (0 = HighGraphics, 1 = dispatcher, at the
+ * entry of its native chain, fix 112); a slot that moves to another thread takes its core along.
+ * The repeat call is two thread-local loads and one global load.
